    "difficulty": "MODERATE",
    "stem": "Evaluate \\(\\int_{-1}^{1} 5x^{4}\\sqrt{x^{5}+1}\\,dx\\).",
    "solution": "Put \\(t = x^{5}+1\\), then \\(dt = 5x^{4}\\,dx\\). Therefore, \\(\\int 5x^{4}\\sqrt{x^{5}+1}\\,dx = \\int \\sqrt{t}\\,dt = \\frac{2}{3}t^{3/2} = \\frac{2}{3}(x^{5}+1)^{3/2}\\). Hence, \\(\\int_{-1}^{1} 5x^{4}\\sqrt{x^{5}+1}\\,dx = \\frac{2}{3}\\left[(x^{5}+1)^{3/2}\\right]_{-1}^{1} = \\frac{2}{3}\\left[(1^{5}+1)^{3/2} - ((-1)^{5}+1)^{3/2}\\right] = \\frac{2}{3}\\left[2^{3/2} - 0^{3/2}\\right] = \\frac{2}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}) = \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\). Alternatively, transform the limits: with \\(t = x^{5}+1\\), when \\(x=-1\\), \\(t=0\\) and when \\(x=1\\), \\(t=2\\). Thus \\(\\int_{-1}^{1} 5x^{4}\\sqrt{x^{5}+1}\\,dx = \\int_{0}^{2} \\sqrt{t}\\,dt = \\frac{2}{3}\\left[t^{3/2}\\right]_{0}^{2} = \\frac{2}{3}\\left[2^{3/2} - 0^{3/2}\\right] = \\frac{2}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}) = \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\)."
  },
